#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105459 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "02451ec6-38a3-5cfc-adfb-912b91fab9dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12382" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n![](images/69/69764cda6db58d51cf12ab33a352191513d3f572292203135108bfc15f902584.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,x({x}^{2}-y)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({x}^{3}-xy)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050816}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be6bfb1-22ec-54d7-8d70-408044da5849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415944} {:answer/id #uuid "11f496da-87e3-5a3b-81b0-ad4ccd99eec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415946} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac43cfe-450e-5f00-a858-df79110cc719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415949} {:answer/id #uuid "855fc78b-5ccc-59f0-bca3-89185d1730e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415960} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c85dc7-b6d4-5404-8564-0781f52fb42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+y+xy)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0be6bfb1-22ec-54d7-8d70-408044da5849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415944} :answer-field/id #uuid "d906cb4e-f500-54c4-87ea-509d8da51301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f27ceb2-3e83-5aa6-8caa-7e1929889c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-161106" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xy,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8797f6aa-cf9f-5cdf-9e2e-81ed04cfb981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{10}(x^{2} + xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102265} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa9edd5-3b13-5153-89a1-cbc230125392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{10}(-x^{2}y + xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102266} {:answer/id #uuid "3c94fbac-ac12-5764-97bd-c8d97a316c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\sqrt{3}(x^{2} - xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102267} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff3c3f4-ebf9-5068-a4a6-bd8e76178c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{5x}(x^{2} + x^{2}y^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102268} {:answer/id #uuid "710da267-89b8-50aa-a066-1c9994d5588d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{5}(x^{2} + 2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102269}] :answer-field/id #uuid "83e432a8-15bc-5c76-a70d-b86380fa6c09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102264}] :question/id #uuid "c6b7ebb0-3a2c-5e35-8e1b-da3b8793e3c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161323" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x, y, z) =-3xz$, where the surface $S$ is defined by $z = \\sqrt{2}x + \\sqrt{2}y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le y \\le 2$.\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below.\n$∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}AAAAAAAdydx$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a533bb1-853f-5491-8c86-67f976471508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275862} {:answer/id #uuid "51369986-90ae-5b8b-bd51-00e4a125fb18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\,x{y}^{2}(y+2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276202} {:answer/id #uuid "62b5397b-e1ef-56af-b3c3-0127ce34e303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}y(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276371} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea26c5b-44a3-5c69-9b86-1ab9c4524f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\,{x}^{2}y(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276627} {:answer/id #uuid "e533f6b6-2b56-5f69-89d2-aa4aa20f904b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a533bb1-853f-5491-8c86-67f976471508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275862} :answer-field/id #uuid "56bb3622-f59e-504a-a378-a8b68a6075e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e62de12f-3877-52f7-8f85-f37913bbcbfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161319" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086549fa-2a26-5001-86f1-5b98b5f931b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275551} {:answer/id #uuid "50ddfd56-d4fe-58e4-9068-9d6c2ddb54e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5x}(2{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276200} {:answer/id #uuid "9dada5e0-74dc-5e81-a533-742d9452a01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276893} {:answer/id #uuid "a82df97c-47b4-5649-adc4-f83468150b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276996} {:answer/id #uuid "bca9a670-f13c-561a-a08a-381f1f64ba75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}({x}^{2}y-x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dada5e0-74dc-5e81-a533-742d9452a01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276893} :answer-field/id #uuid "055d6d61-c143-5db4-98a6-8b0a8ee7ad03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71274639-1883-57e5-9cbd-1e009801625d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161321" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}xyz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}xy(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-2{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2})\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ddc6b9-504b-58c8-bd2c-8ca80ec11d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276397} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed6f32a-ed59-5647-bcd6-86e6da4ee2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{4}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276471} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1689bf-d1a3-5784-9e6f-9117c0c02c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4{x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}x)\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276867} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9b2183-af1c-5150-ab05-abef28325ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277169} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1e7756-7d83-53a3-b74f-dfc6cb5f4b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}{x}^{3}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9b2183-af1c-5150-ab05-abef28325ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277169} :answer-field/id #uuid "60f5e341-969c-5ed5-9a8c-831fa22f5974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26332fd2-3603-5c3f-9cd0-0420c8d9d4db" :question/math-academy-id "q-161325" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(4y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed482a3a-a562-53c9-9952-b7f4ae64a9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401228} {:answer/id #uuid "292ba469-4e9c-5812-b40c-813e27620124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3y}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401229} {:answer/id #uuid "1139e147-84fe-5bb5-a30a-874f5c401de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401230} {:answer/id #uuid "25a694b0-85e4-50a9-9c0d-fb4f6db18268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401231} {:answer/id #uuid "60287cb2-73a4-5d70-9afa-49321e3ddf3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3x}({x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a694b0-85e4-50a9-9c0d-fb4f6db18268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401231} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2d063b-2ed3-5177-a74e-32306bee1975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e5c369-0d01-5527-87a6-9d6d08f8cf0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161314" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x+y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y(-x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fef4addc-f714-5c73-ba79-b8bbf017a6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401235} {:answer/id #uuid "32ada5ea-3508-5fad-8e67-d2275d868693" :answer/t